WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.080
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.300
Hello, everyone.

9
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.680
Welcome to Sabre.

10
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:43.380
Yes, it is Season 6, Episode 15, written by Jennifer Salata, known to us as Jen Salata, and directed by John Krasinski.

11
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.720
Yes, known to us as JK.

12
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.500
Oh, my gosh.

13
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:48.400
John.

14
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.340
Great job.

15
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.100
I loved this episode.

16
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:52.760
I did, too.

17
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.800
It was one of those sleeper ones that I hadn't watched in years.

18
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.180
That I had forgotten about.

19
00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:57.500
Yeah.

20
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.140
And I was cracking up.

21
00:00:59.340 --> 00:00:59.980
Oh, my gosh.

22
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.100
So many memories flooded back for me.

23
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.680
All those daycare scenes.

24
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.820
Oh, they were so good.

25
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:06.840
Oh, my gosh.

26
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.340
I can't wait to talk about them.

27
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.500
I can't wait to talk about them.

28
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.520
And there's even more in the shooting draft that didn't make it in.

29
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.940
So we're going to share it all with you.

30
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.560
Here's a summary.

31
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.420
Dunder Mifflin has officially been bought by a new company, Sabre.

32
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.240
Gabe Lewis, the new director of sales, arrives in Scranton and brings with him

33
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.060
a list of changes and new company policies that do not sit well with Michael.

34
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.500
Michael seeks advice from a very unemployed David Wallace.

35
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.780
Meanwhile, Pam and Jim tour a daycare center, and Aaron and Andy wait for the other to make

36
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.260
a move.

37
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.420
It is the most awkward courtship of two people.

38
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.100
I was yelling at the TV screen.

39
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.480
I was like, one of you just asked the other one out.

40
00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.700
They're making me crazy.

41
00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.320
We have so much stuff to share in this episode.

42
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.580
I mean, I hope we can get through it all.

43
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:04.940
We better get to it.

44
00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.260
But before we do, folks, I want you to know Jenna's wearing a poofy flower on her lapel today.

45
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:10.840
Okay.

46
00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.860
I'm trying this.

47
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.400
This is your book look.

48
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.000
Oh, and our book is coming out in two weeks.

49
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.140
In two weeks.

50
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:23.360
Our book is launching into the world, and we need a bunch of outfits, Angela.

51
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:30.220
You know I've been spending weeks and weeks curating outfits because I am not naturally

52
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.220
gifted in that area.

53
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.540
I never want you to stop trying to put together outfits because now every day, and I'm talking,

54
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.700
I mean, almost every day, I get a picture of you in front of your closet.

55
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:44.760
And you're like, what about this?

56
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:45.500
I know.

57
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.060
And so here's the problem I'm having.

58
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.200
I declared that I was going to wear fakie flowers.

59
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.380
You sure did.

60
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:53.880
You made a big declaration.

61
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.240
And it's not working.

62
00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.280
I'm wearing one today because I wanted you to see.

63
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.040
Hear me out.

64
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:00.840
I'm going to stand up.

65
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.080
Well, I want you to see what I think is happening here.

66
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.220
And I know it's the striped shirt, but I think it's the fakie flower too.

67
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.280
Are you ready?

68
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:09.720
Yeah.

69
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.460
What does this remind you of?

70
00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:14.860
Oh, yeah.

71
00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.300
You look like a French mime.

72
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.080
I look like a Parisian mime.

73
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:18.400
You do.

74
00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.800
Wait, I need a picture of you doing that.

75
00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.170
Oh, my gosh.

76
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:25.950
All right.

77
00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:26.990
Well, we're very excited.

78
00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:28.430
Our book is coming out in two weeks.

79
00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.370
We can't believe it.

80
00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.970
I can't promise I'm going to wear a fakie flower, everyone.

81
00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.210
I'm sorry to disappoint.

82
00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.190
I'm still trying.

83
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.670
You got to have one, Jenna.

84
00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:36.290
Just one.

85
00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:37.470
I'll work on it.

86
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:38.710
All right.

87
00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.370
Let's get into fast facts.

88
00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:45.630
Fast fact number one, John Krasinski directed this episode.

89
00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:46.950
My gosh.

90
00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.390
We had a fan question from Alice R.

91
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:50.010
in Italy.

92
00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:50.890
No.

93
00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:56.570
How was it seeing John Krasinski on the other side of the camera as director?

94
00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.950
He did an amazing job.

95
00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.090
He sure did.

96
00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.610
He was just like a natural.

97
00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:02.990
He really was.

98
00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.790
It was a total pleasure.

99
00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.410
It was always tricky.

100
00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:11.030
What was it going to be like when one of our castmates stepped into directing?

101
00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:14.970
Particularly because they had to be on both sides of the camera.

102
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.050
Like, logistically, how does that work?

103
00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:17.630
Right.

104
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.590
And John was your major scene partner.

105
00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:20.310
He was.

106
00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.730
It was so crazy how we could do scenes together.

107
00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.930
And he would be completely in character as Jim.

108
00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.690
I felt like I was talking to Jim.

109
00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:34.330
But then we would cut and he would have simultaneously observed things and would have notes.

110
00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.450
I don't know how he did.

111
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:36.590
I don't know how your brain does that.

112
00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:37.190
My brain doesn't do that.

113
00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.590
Some people have that brain, though.

114
00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:42.990
They just compartmentalize it and then they know how to like, I'm sort of a one trick pony.

115
00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:43.910
I know.

116
00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.290
I like to be doing the one thing artistically.

117
00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.150
I'm real good at the one thing at one time.

118
00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.850
Well, we reached out to John and he's off right now filming season three of Jack Ryan.

119
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:58.950
But he sent in some audio clips and we're so excited to share them with you.

120
00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.710
First off, he talked about what it was like to direct the show.

121
00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.630
Hi, guys.

122
00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:05.770
Wait a minute.

123
00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.690
How are you on Saber already?

124
00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.450
That means that it is season six?

125
00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.190
You're into season six?

126
00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:13.730
No.

127
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.250
Slow down.

128
00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.110
We need more of this podcaSt.

129
00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.330
The world needs more of this podcast for the love of Pete.

130
00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:22.170
That's all I have to say about that.

131
00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.990
What was it like directing Saber?

132
00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.910
It was a dream.

133
00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.850
It was absolutely everything you would wish directing a show like this would be.

134
00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.910
I personally was terrified because I had to direct all of you.

135
00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:40.870
I remember being really, really nervous about that, that you were my co-workers, but you were

136
00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.570
my closest friends, you were my family.

137
00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:49.150
And here I was thinking I had the audacity to tell you what to do, which incidentally,

138
00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.230
I don't think I did once.

139
00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:54.950
I'm not sure I gave one acting note to any of you because I was just in awe watching,

140
00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.690
sitting back and watching, which really was one of the most special parts about directing

141
00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:05.370
the show, was getting to see through a totally unique lens of being behind these monitors,

142
00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:11.630
the magic that every single one of you brought to each and every moment, whether it was a

143
00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.650
joke or whether it was an emotional beat.

144
00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.270
Everyone was just so good at what they did.

145
00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.610
And of course, as an actor, I always used to love watching everybody play in these scenes,

146
00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.250
but there were so many scenes that I wasn't a part of.

147
00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:31.610
So, you know, getting to in Saber, getting to be a part of the hot tub scene with Michael

148
00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:32.910
and David Wallace.

149
00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:39.390
At the end of the day, I think that directing that episode was a much bigger deal than I

150
00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:43.390
even understood or imagined then in that moment.

151
00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.550
Because the truth is, directing is terrifying.

152
00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:51.530
As I told you, I was very nervous to do it on this show.

153
00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:58.350
And honestly, if it hadn't been for this episode being such an amazing experience, surrounded

154
00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:04.690
by such hugely supportive crew members, cast members, producers, everyone, I don't know

155
00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:05.910
that I ever would have done it again.

156
00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:06.790
Truly.

157
00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:11.070
I mean, I think it was something that I was really excited to experiment with on our show,

158
00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:15.910
but never thought that I'd go much further than that in trying to direct anything else.

159
00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:17.770
Can you imagine?

160
00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.150
Oh, man.

161
00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.430
John, could you imagine if he didn't keep directing?

162
00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:22.470
He's so good.

163
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:23.150
I know.

164
00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:28.850
I just love that John got to have this experience on our show for the first time because you

165
00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.190
and I know how loving and supportive everyone was.

166
00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.770
This was true for other people as well.

167
00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:39.210
I mean, our editor, Claire Scanlon, has a very successful directing career in television.

168
00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:40.690
That started with The Office.

169
00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:46.990
That was because Greg was very encouraging of having different department heads step into

170
00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.730
that role of director to step out of what they were doing and try this new thing in a really

171
00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:52.710
supportive environment.

172
00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.050
I feel like it launched the directing careers of many people.

173
00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:56.490
Mm-hmm.

174
00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.710
Randall Einhorn, our cinematographer.

175
00:07:58.890 --> 00:07:59.510
Matt Sohn.

176
00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:00.030
Yes.

177
00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:00.610
So many.

178
00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.210
And, by the way, the writer of this episode, Jen Salata.

179
00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:04.550
Yeah.

180
00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.650
I was just watching television the other day.

181
00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:09.290
I was watching Abbott Elementary, and she directed the episode.

182
00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:10.710
I was like, oh my gosh.

183
00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:10.930
Yeah.

184
00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:11.390
Jen.

185
00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:12.750
So many people.

186
00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:19.390
Well, listen, speaking of Jen Salata, there is a great interview with her and John over

187
00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.690
on officetelly.com for this episode.

188
00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:26.010
I'll share some of what they talked about as we go along, but one of the things that it

189
00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:31.090
included were interviews with cast members about John's directing.

190
00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.110
Oh, really?

191
00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:32.590
Yes.

192
00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:34.150
So I'm going to tell you what people said.

193
00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:34.490
All right.

194
00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:35.850
Ellie said,

195
00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:50.590
Brian said he was bold, which I thought was cool.

196
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:52.750
He wanted characters to try new things.

197
00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:54.930
Kate said,

198
00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:59.030
John was a very thoughtful director who created a great tone on set.

199
00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:01.370
He really did his homework on all levels.

200
00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.170
He took his job very seriously, but not himself.

201
00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.110
Like, he didn't take himself too seriously.

202
00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:07.030
That's John.

203
00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:08.490
And then Creed said,

204
00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:09.870
which one's John?

205
00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:11.310
Oh my gosh, Creed.

206
00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:12.890
Creed with the button.

207
00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:13.970
So perfect.

208
00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:15.210
All right.

209
00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:16.810
Fast fact number two.

210
00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:21.210
I'll have you know, this was not John's directorial debut.

211
00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:22.470
What was?

212
00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.410
So this might've been the thing that inspired him to keep directing, but it was not his debut.

213
00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.610
He had written, produced, and directed the film Brief Interviews with Hideous Men,

214
00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:35.310
based on the short story collection by David Foster Wallace.

215
00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:36.990
I remember this.

216
00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:41.190
From the time we did the pilot, John loved this book.

217
00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:48.050
And I guess he purchased the rights to the book with his first paycheck from the office.

218
00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:49.870
He had always wanted to adapt it.

219
00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.250
He's so smart like that.

220
00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:51.970
I know.

221
00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:53.530
He's such a forward thinker.

222
00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:54.170
He is.

223
00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:57.090
Originally, he was just going to write and produce the film.

224
00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.970
Did you know John majored in screenwriting at Brown University?

225
00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:02.270
I didn't know that.

226
00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:03.150
Yeah.

227
00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.270
So he majored in screenwriting, not acting.

228
00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:08.310
Writing was really his first love.

229
00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.110
So anyway, he had the rights, but he couldn't find a director.

230
00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:19.170
And I guess it was Rainn Wilson who said to him on set one day, why don't you just direct it yourself?

231
00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:20.890
That sounds like Rain, too.

232
00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:24.110
Rainn is very much the person that's like, why don't you just do it?

233
00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:25.590
And you're like, oh, well, okay.

234
00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:26.470
Yeah.

235
00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:32.250
So I guess Rainn Wilson kind of kick-started John's directing career.

236
00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.470
The next thing he directed was this episode of The Office.

237
00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:38.510
Then he directed two more episodes of The Office.

238
00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:39.450
So three total.

239
00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.250
Then when The Office was over, he directed a movie called The Hollers.

240
00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.770
And of course, A Quiet Place.

241
00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.950
Which I watched on the plane.

242
00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:48.810
Oh, lady.

243
00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:49.430
Was I crazy?

244
00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:50.290
Oh, lady.

245
00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:50.890
I jumped out of my seat.

246
00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:52.010
Did you yell out?

247
00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:52.670
Yes.

248
00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:53.590
I was like, ah!

249
00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:55.950
Like, I'm sure people thought I was crazy.

250
00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:57.150
Oh, my gosh.

251
00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:02.270
Well, you know, his next directorial project is a film called If.

252
00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:04.510
And he's reuniting with Steve Carell.

253
00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:05.610
Steve's going to be in it.

254
00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:06.510
Oh, my gosh.

255
00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:07.690
We have to go see that together.

256
00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:08.790
Let's do it.

257
00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.950
So one more thing I want to point out before we move on to Fast Fact No.

258
00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:13.130
3.

259
00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:14.070
Because Fast Fact No.

