Oh, my God.

1276
00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:45.780
So, Jenna, we all got an email during this time that there wouldn't be plastic water bottles on set.

1277
00:56:45.960 --> 00:56:46.120
Yes, yes.

1278
00:56:46.140 --> 00:56:49.800
That we were all getting a stainless steel water bottle.

1279
00:56:49.940 --> 00:56:50.080
Yep.

1280
00:56:50.260 --> 00:56:51.720
That said Dunder Mifflin on it.

1281
00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:52.000
Yep.

1282
00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:54.220
And we were going to refill them throughout the day.

1283
00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:57.260
So, Jenna, you remembered so quickly it was Paul.

1284
00:56:57.420 --> 00:57:03.000
I couldn't remember if it was Paul or Brent because I remember Brent was very much about being healthy and eco-friendly.

1285
00:57:03.280 --> 00:57:03.460
Yes.

1286
00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:04.620
I texted Brent.

1287
00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:05.400
Brent, was this you?

1288
00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:07.560
He was like, no, I'm pretty sure it was Paul.

1289
00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:12.180
So, then I emailed Paul and I said, Paul, I know we did this, right?

1290
00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:13.480
I found in my digital clutter.

1291
00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:14.500
Was this your idea?

1292
00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:19.740
And was it also part of the inspiration for Gabe and his Saber water bottles?

1293
00:57:19.900 --> 00:57:21.440
And this is what Paul had to say.

1294
00:57:21.700 --> 00:57:25.600
Paul said, yes, I tried to get us to be plastic free.

1295
00:57:25.820 --> 00:57:27.100
It worked for a little while.

1296
00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:28.620
I remember that, too.

1297
00:57:28.640 --> 00:57:30.120
It was very short-lived.

1298
00:57:30.160 --> 00:57:30.540
I know.

1299
00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:38.520
He said, as far as it being an intended storyline for Gabe, he doesn't have a clear memory that that was specifically a Gabe storyline.

1300
00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:43.640
But in general, so much of what was happening around us was inspiration for the show.

1301
00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:44.540
All right.

1302
00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:46.740
It was probably connected.

1303
00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:49.180
But I was like, oh, my gosh, we did this.

1304
00:57:49.380 --> 00:57:50.980
Do you know what I remembered?

1305
00:57:51.320 --> 00:57:51.540
What?

1306
00:57:51.940 --> 00:57:53.440
Standing in line to refill it.

1307
00:57:53.580 --> 00:57:55.240
There literally was a line.

1308
00:57:55.380 --> 00:57:55.660
Yes.

1309
00:57:55.660 --> 00:58:01.540
Well, you know, Paul told me that this was sort of a craze that was happening during this time.

1310
00:58:02.140 --> 00:58:05.100
Companies were really trying to limit plastic use.

1311
00:58:05.260 --> 00:58:08.260
And so he wanted us to be a part of that, you know?

1312
00:58:08.260 --> 00:58:08.620
Yes.

1313
00:58:08.740 --> 00:58:10.540
I do think it's a great idea.

1314
00:58:11.180 --> 00:58:15.400
But I think you need more than one refill jug area.

1315
00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:17.460
That probably would have helped.

1316
00:58:17.520 --> 00:58:18.260
That would have helped.

1317
00:58:19.760 --> 00:58:23.540
Well, Michael has a talking head about all this where he says,

1318
00:58:23.540 --> 00:58:29.760
okay, there's a small part of him that's excited about the new company, but 70% of him is water.

1319
00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:35.560
And at the end of this talking head, he says that he thinks these changes suck ball.

1320
00:58:36.520 --> 00:58:38.140
Because you know what he was going to say.

1321
00:58:38.440 --> 00:58:39.260
Sucks balls.

1322
00:58:39.460 --> 00:58:40.260
It was so funny.

1323
00:58:40.500 --> 00:58:45.480
I guess cutting off the end of Michael saying balls was an idea that John had.

1324
00:58:45.640 --> 00:58:48.080
He came up with it in editing with Dave Rogers.

1325
00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:50.460
And I love their take on this.

1326
00:58:50.460 --> 00:58:54.560
So what they were saying was that, obviously, Michael completed his sentence.

1327
00:58:55.060 --> 00:59:03.240
But they imagined that when the documentary aired, that the documentarians would have left in all of the footage.

1328
00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:07.820
But the network running the documentary would have to cut it off.

1329
00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:08.520
Right.

1330
00:59:08.820 --> 00:59:11.000
Because of, you know, standards and practices.

1331
00:59:11.000 --> 00:59:18.840
And he said they based that on the amount of times that we had to cut things or change things because of standards and practices.

1332
00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:20.740
Every episode, probably.

1333
00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:21.760
Very realistic.

1334
00:59:22.140 --> 00:59:26.980
Well, listen, Angela, you mentioned the line to fill their water bottles.

1335
00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:30.740
And while Michael is in line, Toby's getting water and it runs out.

1336
00:59:30.860 --> 00:59:32.400
Toby says we kicked it.

1337
00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:33.720
Which cracked me up.

1338
00:59:33.760 --> 00:59:34.280
Me too.

1339
00:59:34.280 --> 00:59:37.160
I wrote it down at 8 minutes 45 seconds.

1340
00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:40.040
That's what you say when a keg runs out, right?

1341
00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:40.640
I know.

1342
00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:43.360
Well, what does that tell us about Toby?

1343
00:59:43.360 --> 00:59:45.840
Well, I think, what does it tell us about Paul?

1344
00:59:46.060 --> 00:59:50.800
Because I've bought a pony keg with Paul and we've kicked it.

1345
00:59:51.020 --> 00:59:56.540
See, I would never use that language because I've never purchased a keg and I've never had a beer out of a keg.

1346
00:59:56.860 --> 00:59:57.520
Oh, man.

1347
00:59:57.680 --> 00:59:58.660
I'm not a beer person.

1348
00:59:58.960 --> 00:59:59.240
Listen.

1349
00:59:59.580 --> 01:00:04.140
Now, I'll do a flavored ale or a cider beer.

1350
01:00:04.420 --> 01:00:09.780
A really, really cold keg and like getting a beer out of a super cold keg.

1351
01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:11.720
It's great, especially like on a hot day.

1352
01:00:11.980 --> 01:00:16.300
Maybe you put one, you know, at second base because you're playing softball with friends.

1353
01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:22.780
And if you make it to second base, you get a beer and you get a little tooted while you're waiting to run into home plate.

1354
01:00:23.440 --> 01:00:25.820
That cold beer out of a keg.

1355
01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:26.760
It's amazing.

1356
01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:28.940
I mean, that sounds miserable to me.

1357
01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:33.040
You drink some beer and then you have to run on your sloshy beer stomach.

1358
01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:37.720
Have you seen like co-ed softball, like with a group of friends?

1359
01:00:37.720 --> 01:00:39.200
This is not like real athleticism.

1360
01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:42.060
You have lived part of life that I haven't lived.

1361
01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:44.180
You've had all these experiences.

1362
01:00:44.400 --> 01:00:48.040
And to me, it's like you lived in a foreign land.

1363
01:00:48.600 --> 01:00:48.840
What?

1364
01:00:49.040 --> 01:00:51.220
A foreign land of softball and beer?

1365
01:00:51.420 --> 01:00:51.780
Yeah.

1366
01:00:52.140 --> 01:00:55.440
I have no like frame of reference for that.

1367
01:00:55.640 --> 01:00:57.960
I know you and Lee bond over this stuff all the time.

1368
01:00:58.080 --> 01:00:59.160
Is this a Texas thing?

1369
01:00:59.320 --> 01:01:00.920
I mean, I'm sure they're doing it in Missouri.

1370
01:01:00.920 --> 01:01:01.880
Where was I?

1371
01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:03.660
What was I doing during this time?

1372
01:01:03.660 --> 01:01:07.260
This was just like a hip young person out in the world.

1373
01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:08.900
I've never been a hip young person.

1374
01:01:09.000 --> 01:01:09.620
Well, that's true.

1375
01:01:09.700 --> 01:01:12.620
I've been a 65-year-old woman since the day I was born.

1376
01:01:12.760 --> 01:01:13.240
It's true.

1377
01:01:13.240 --> 01:01:14.320
And I love you for it.

1378
01:01:14.360 --> 01:01:14.960
Thank you, lady.

1379
01:01:15.240 --> 01:01:18.060
As I stare at your big fake flower that's on your lapel.

1380
01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:19.360
I know it's not working.

1381
01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:21.100
I know it's not working.

1382
01:01:21.740 --> 01:01:27.900
I wore it in today because I wanted to prove to you that this cannot happen on our book launch tour.

1383
01:01:28.580 --> 01:01:29.320
It can't.

1384
01:01:29.420 --> 01:01:29.920
Look at me.

1385
01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:30.800
It's ridiculous.

1386
01:01:31.260 --> 01:01:32.060
You never had to.

1387
01:01:32.060 --> 01:01:33.720
Is water coming out of this flower?

1388
01:01:33.880 --> 01:01:34.660
Squirt, squirt.

1389
01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:36.140
It's borderline clownish.

1390
01:01:36.140 --> 01:01:37.160
It can't happen.

1391
01:01:37.380 --> 01:01:39.160
You never had to prove it to me.

1392
01:01:39.840 --> 01:01:41.640
I've known it from the beginning.

1393
01:01:41.860 --> 01:01:44.060
Well, I needed to prove it to myself.

1394
01:01:44.500 --> 01:01:45.260
All right.

1395
01:01:45.460 --> 01:01:46.400
Where are we?

1396
01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:50.520
Well, Toby has just kicked the water cooler.

1397
01:01:50.760 --> 01:01:51.360
Oh, yes.

1398
01:01:51.480 --> 01:01:52.040
That's right.

1399
01:01:52.380 --> 01:01:54.540
Well, now Michael's going to confront Gabe.

1400
01:01:54.900 --> 01:01:57.620
He's going to say, listen, I don't mean to seem ungrateful,

1401
01:01:57.620 --> 01:02:02.220
but I am really struggling to embrace some of these changes, such as all of them.

1402
01:02:03.880 --> 01:02:06.560
Gabe says it's kind of above his pay grade.

1403
01:02:06.800 --> 01:02:11.660
He can't really change any of the policies, but he'll see what he can do.

1404
01:02:12.080 --> 01:02:17.340
And now Pam and Jim are going to enter the daycare center.

1405
01:02:17.340 --> 01:02:21.260
All of these scenes were absolutely delicious.

1406
01:02:21.500 --> 01:02:23.560
I loved watching them.

1407
01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:25.840
We had a fan question from Alice R.

1408
01:02:25.940 --> 01:02:26.400
in Italy.

1409
01:02:26.660 --> 01:02:28.960
Where did you shoot the daycare scenes?

1410
01:02:29.120 --> 01:02:30.420
Was it a real daycare?

1411
01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:32.260
Did you make any changes?

1412
01:02:33.180 --> 01:02:35.040
Lady, did you recognize it?

1413
01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:35.960
No.

1414
01:02:36.580 --> 01:02:36.860
Okay.

1415
01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:39.320
Well, it's called Sandbox Daycare.

1416
01:02:39.400 --> 01:02:40.200
We made that up.

1417
01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:42.500
That's not even a real daycare in Scranton.

1418
01:02:42.500 --> 01:02:47.200
And we shot this at Peekaboo Playland in Eagle Rock, California.

1419
01:02:47.660 --> 01:02:50.080
Did you not take Isabel to Peekaboo Playland?

1420
01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:52.460
Was that after she was a toddler?

1421
01:02:52.780 --> 01:02:55.280
This was all the rage when my kids were little.

1422
01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:56.960
Eagle Rock's far from me.

1423
01:02:57.060 --> 01:02:58.220
I guess that's true.

1424
01:02:58.540 --> 01:03:02.260
We had tons and tons of Playland-type places right by me,

1425
01:03:02.280 --> 01:03:04.160
so I never needed to venture this far.

1426
01:03:04.380 --> 01:03:07.240
Well, you know, when I did this episode, I didn't have kids.

1427
01:03:07.240 --> 01:03:10.260
But then when I was re-watching it, I was like, it's Peekaboo!

1428
01:03:10.260 --> 01:03:11.760
Oh, it's Peekaboo Playland!

1429
01:03:11.860 --> 01:03:15.320
I've spent so many days in this space!

1430
01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:17.160
See, the one by me is the coop.

1431
01:03:17.400 --> 01:03:19.080
I spent tons of time at the coop.

1432
01:03:19.260 --> 01:03:20.140
I've been to the coop.

1433
01:03:20.340 --> 01:03:20.500
Yeah.

1434
01:03:21.080 --> 01:03:23.960
All right, so there's a big twirly slide in the middle.

1435
01:03:24.260 --> 01:03:27.120
There's like a treehouse around the curly slide.

1436
01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:28.680
It's the cutest dang thing.

1437
01:03:29.260 --> 01:03:32.780
When we get there, Jim and Pam are totally charmed,

1438
01:03:34.100 --> 01:03:38.940
except Jim accidentally walks in on the daycare director

1439
01:03:38.940 --> 01:03:41.100
who is using the little kitty toilet.

1440
01:03:41.540 --> 01:03:43.620
Those toilets are so low to the ground.

1441
01:03:43.980 --> 01:03:46.840
Well, they need to be because of the little children.

1442
01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:49.480
I know, but seeing a grown man on it really did.

1443
01:03:50.300 --> 01:03:51.540
I was like, oh my God!

1444
01:03:53.500 --> 01:03:57.280
John sent us in an audio clip of what it was like to shoot in the daycare.

1445
01:03:57.520 --> 01:03:58.540
Here's what he had to say.

1446
01:03:59.180 --> 01:04:02.180
My other favorite memory from that episode

1447
01:04:02.180 --> 01:04:05.340
was the interview at the daycare center.

1448
01:04:05.540 --> 01:04:07.260
Jenna, as we've talked about many times,

1449
01:04:07.400 --> 01:04:08.920
and I won't embarrass you on your own show,

1450
01:04:09.100 --> 01:04:10.500
I apologize, but not really.

1451
01:04:11.160 --> 01:04:14.380
Truly some of my favorite, favorite memories in all of my career

1452
01:04:14.380 --> 01:04:16.180
were getting to do scenes with you, period.

1453
01:04:16.780 --> 01:04:22.940
But especially those scenes where the two of us were sort of stuck in it together,

1454
01:04:23.120 --> 01:04:25.560
that we were both in something that we had to get out of together.

1455
01:04:25.560 --> 01:04:28.240
I remember very specifically your line reading

1456
01:04:28.240 --> 01:04:32.260
when I came out from seeing Jerry in the bathroom.

1457
01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:34.840
And I said, I just saw someone in the bathroom.

1458
01:04:35.120 --> 01:04:38.460
And you very earnestly ask, who was it?

1459
01:04:38.580 --> 01:04:39.320
Who was it?

1460
01:04:39.380 --> 01:04:42.260
And then you quickly digress as your brain realizes,

1461
01:04:42.520 --> 01:04:44.960
oh, it was definitely the guy that we're about to interview.

1462
01:04:45.140 --> 01:04:47.120
And your line reading of who was it?

1463
01:04:47.180 --> 01:04:47.660
Who was it?

1464
01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:49.520
Was it the guy who's going to interview us?

1465
01:04:49.620 --> 01:04:51.880
It was so funny.

1466
01:04:51.880 --> 01:04:56.580
And I don't know that I had a good enough take of my reaction to you.

1467
01:04:56.760 --> 01:04:59.900
In fact, do I physically bail out after you say that to me?

1468
01:05:00.600 --> 01:05:02.460
Man, that really made me laugh.

1469
01:05:02.620 --> 01:05:05.480
I seem to remember going through, I don't know,

1470
01:05:05.680 --> 01:05:09.360
10, 12 takes of just not being able to keep it together.

1471
01:05:09.680 --> 01:05:13.060
That was a total GiggleFest day for me.

1472
01:05:13.180 --> 01:05:16.640
You were always more professional, but I was in full GiggleFest mode.

1473
01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:19.700
And shout out to my friend, Joey Slotnick, who played Jerry.

1474
01:05:19.700 --> 01:05:21.660
I was so excited to get him on the show.

1475
01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:23.480
And he absolutely crushed it.

1476
01:05:23.720 --> 01:05:29.000
That gesture he does in the bathroom where he silently raises his hand.

1477
01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:33.580
But there is no, there is nothing said between the two people.

1478
01:05:34.100 --> 01:05:37.600
Just utter shame and embarrassment and guilt.

1479
01:05:37.860 --> 01:05:39.360
He was so great on that show.

1480
01:05:39.480 --> 01:05:42.680
And I just remember yet again, just getting to watch those scenes

1481
01:05:42.680 --> 01:05:44.160
and not have to give any notes.

1482
01:05:45.140 --> 01:05:50.060
All right, so I just have to say, I totally remember that.

1483
01:05:50.200 --> 01:05:53.480
Yes, John, you do physically bail out of the scene.

1484
01:05:53.980 --> 01:05:55.540
But also, you know what?

1485
01:05:55.620 --> 01:05:59.340
I really appreciate him saying that he liked shooting these scenes with me

1486
01:05:59.340 --> 01:06:04.420
because I think there's often a lot written about our romantic chemistry on screen.

1487
01:06:04.700 --> 01:06:08.280
But we had a really special comedic chemistry together.

1488
01:06:08.280 --> 01:06:11.900
And I feel like this storyline really highlights that.

1489
01:06:12.060 --> 01:06:14.200
We were great partners all around.

1490
01:06:14.520 --> 01:06:15.360
I agree.

1491
01:06:15.360 --> 01:06:20.440
And I loved what John said about when you guys were both in a sticky situation together.

1492
01:06:21.000 --> 01:06:22.640
Yes, I loved it.

1493
01:06:23.020 --> 01:06:27.780
And, you know, John mentioned Jerry, the daycare director, was played by Joey Slotnick.

1494
01:06:27.880 --> 01:06:30.340
He was a really good friend of John's.

1495
01:06:30.560 --> 01:06:34.040
He had also been in John's movie, Brief Interviews with Hideous Men.

1496
01:06:34.040 --> 01:06:38.440
But Joey had actually auditioned for this part through Alison Jones.

1497
01:06:38.740 --> 01:06:41.360
I guess he'd auditioned for other roles on the show.

1498
01:06:41.640 --> 01:06:45.060
And when this role came up, she thought of him.

1499
01:06:45.200 --> 01:06:46.540
And she knew he was John's friend.

1500
01:06:47.160 --> 01:06:53.980
But you might recognize Joey from the TV shows Alias, Boston Public, The Single Guy, or Nip Tuck.

1501
01:06:54.080 --> 01:06:57.940
He was also in the movies Twister and The Secret Life of Walter Mitty,

1502
01:06:58.280 --> 01:07:01.680
which, by the way, is one of my favorite movies directed by Ben Stiller.

1503
01:07:01.680 --> 01:07:04.880
Well, I met Joey years ago when I first came to Los Angeles.

1504
01:07:04.880 --> 01:07:09.380
My friend got me a job just for one season being a stand-in on The Single Guy.

1505
01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:11.560
I didn't know you were a stand-in.

1506
01:07:11.700 --> 01:07:13.320
Yeah, just for one season.

1507
01:07:13.560 --> 01:07:13.820
Still.

1508
01:07:14.100 --> 01:07:15.120
It was so cool.

1509
01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:17.980
What a great way to learn the ins and outs of a four-camera show.

1510
01:07:18.200 --> 01:07:18.640
Yes.

1511
01:07:18.780 --> 01:07:22.520
You know, I did background work for years when I first came out.

1512
01:07:22.640 --> 01:07:22.900
Same.

1513
01:07:23.200 --> 01:07:27.380
A great way to just sit on a set and observe how it works.

1514
01:07:27.380 --> 01:07:32.980
So by the time I finally booked a role and I had a line, I was like, oh, I know the flow.

1515
01:07:33.160 --> 01:07:34.600
Like, I know some of the language.

1516
01:07:34.880 --> 01:07:35.820
I know this world.

1517
01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:39.860
And in four-camera stand-ins walk through the role while they're lighting.

1518
01:07:40.120 --> 01:07:41.800
They say the lines that go to the mark.

1519
