And she said she thought it was perfect because since he's been home with his kids more often,

2026
01:29:47.600 --> 01:29:50.520
he was probably frustrated with seeing their toys everywhere.

2027
01:29:50.520 --> 01:29:51.860
It felt very organic.

2028
01:29:52.200 --> 01:29:53.720
But how adorable is that?

2029
01:29:54.120 --> 01:29:54.940
So cute.

2030
01:29:54.940 --> 01:29:58.940
Well, lady, I decided to Google, how do you suck up toys?

2031
01:29:59.280 --> 01:29:59.740
Okay.

2032
01:30:00.140 --> 01:30:03.700
I found an article by Newsweek in March of 2022.

2033
01:30:04.420 --> 01:30:09.440
It talked about a guy who built a real-life Suck It for Lego pieces.

2034
01:30:10.260 --> 01:30:10.860
Whoa.

2035
01:30:11.180 --> 01:30:12.200
How cool is that?

2036
01:30:12.380 --> 01:30:16.360
His name is Matty Benedetto, and he posted the video.

2037
01:30:16.360 --> 01:30:21.920
It shows him using a custom vacuum to collect pieces of Lego on the floor.

2038
01:30:22.200 --> 01:30:23.220
Here's the ad.

2039
01:30:23.940 --> 01:30:28.640
I invented the Lego Suck It, the world's first vacuum-powered Lego cleanup device

2040
01:30:28.640 --> 01:30:31.860
that automatically sorts each brick by its individual size.

2041
01:30:32.120 --> 01:30:36.260
This vibrant cleaning accessory features a multistage chamber that magnetically separates

2042
01:30:36.260 --> 01:30:38.820
for quick organization of your favorite toy bricks.

2043
01:30:39.080 --> 01:30:42.440
The high-suction vacuum will ensure every brick is sucked up from your floors

2044
01:30:42.440 --> 01:30:44.540
and swiftly deposited into the clear tube

2045
01:30:44.540 --> 01:30:48.480
as each Lego piece cascades down and gets sorted into their different sizes.

2046
01:30:48.840 --> 01:30:51.720
So when things get messy, just suck it.

2047
01:30:54.640 --> 01:30:55.080
Wow.

2048
01:30:55.400 --> 01:30:56.920
How many Legos have you stepped on?

2049
01:30:57.040 --> 01:30:57.860
It's so painful.

2050
01:30:58.180 --> 01:31:02.020
But how are you not sucking up everything on the floor?

2051
01:31:02.240 --> 01:31:05.080
Like, how is there not also a goldfish in there, maybe?

2052
01:31:05.360 --> 01:31:06.580
Oh, or a Cheerio.

2053
01:31:06.760 --> 01:31:07.180
Exactly.

2054
01:31:07.340 --> 01:31:07.600
Right.

2055
01:31:07.740 --> 01:31:08.320
I don't know.

2056
01:31:08.420 --> 01:31:10.000
You have to have a clean Lego space.

2057
01:31:10.200 --> 01:31:11.140
This is the thing.

2058
01:31:11.240 --> 01:31:16.620
This is kind of the problem with David's invention, which is that, you know,

2059
01:31:16.620 --> 01:31:21.900
yes, you're going to suck up the toys, but how do you avoid sucking up everything else?

2060
01:31:22.260 --> 01:31:23.340
You just suck it.

2061
01:31:23.480 --> 01:31:24.360
You just suck it.

2062
01:31:24.440 --> 01:31:25.020
You just suck it.

2063
01:31:26.460 --> 01:31:29.040
Well, Michael's going to leave David Wallace's.

2064
01:31:29.140 --> 01:31:30.700
He's so defeated.

2065
01:31:31.180 --> 01:31:36.320
He says very few things would make him say no to working with David Wallace, but suck it is one of them.

2066
01:31:36.320 --> 01:31:50.400
And then I love this shot of Michael slowly pulling away, and David Wallace is sort of like lumbering down the driveway toward the car, like kind of like a zombie.

2067
01:31:50.600 --> 01:31:52.420
It reminded me of a zombie movie.

2068
01:31:53.560 --> 01:32:00.180
Jen Salata said this shot was John's idea, and I think it is some foreshadowing for A Quiet Place.

2069
01:32:00.320 --> 01:32:00.920
Oh, really?

2070
01:32:01.060 --> 01:32:01.680
I really do.

2071
01:32:01.800 --> 01:32:03.340
It felt very horror movie to me.

2072
01:32:03.660 --> 01:32:10.980
Right after this creepy goodbye with David Wallace would have come the final accounting scene in the runner that got deleted.

2073
01:32:11.640 --> 01:32:13.420
Here's how it wraps up.

2074
01:32:13.860 --> 01:32:15.900
Accounting area, interior office.

2075
01:32:16.200 --> 01:32:18.200
Angela's looking over Kevin's work.

2076
01:32:18.420 --> 01:32:20.240
Angela, who did this?

2077
01:32:20.600 --> 01:32:21.780
Oscar shakes his head.

2078
01:32:22.420 --> 01:32:25.300
Kevin, it's possible I did it on my own.

2079
01:32:25.600 --> 01:32:27.700
Angela, no, it's not.

2080
01:32:28.020 --> 01:32:30.680
That is the one thing that is not possible.

2081
01:32:30.940 --> 01:32:31.920
How did this happen?

2082
01:32:32.540 --> 01:32:34.380
Kevin, I did it.

2083
01:32:34.820 --> 01:32:37.440
Angela sighs, confused, and walks away.

2084
01:32:37.640 --> 01:32:41.320
Just missing Todd, who approaches with Kevin's adding machine.

2085
01:32:41.820 --> 01:32:42.780
Kevin looks nervous.

2086
01:32:43.420 --> 01:32:45.680
Todd, you left this on my desk.

2087
01:32:45.920 --> 01:32:46.960
Kevin, thanks.

2088
01:32:47.620 --> 01:32:49.860
Todd and Oscar ad-lib hellos.

2089
01:32:50.100 --> 01:32:51.320
And Todd leaves.

2090
01:32:51.320 --> 01:32:53.820
Kevin, don't tell Angela.

2091
01:32:54.260 --> 01:32:55.720
Oscar, I won't.

2092
01:32:56.120 --> 01:32:58.600
Kevin, I didn't just let him do the work.

2093
01:32:58.720 --> 01:32:59.480
I learned stuff.

2094
01:32:59.700 --> 01:33:00.600
Please don't tell her.

2095
01:33:00.940 --> 01:33:02.820
Oscar, I said I won't.

2096
01:33:03.260 --> 01:33:04.380
Kevin looks nervous.

2097
01:33:04.920 --> 01:33:05.980
Oscar looks up at him.

2098
01:33:06.380 --> 01:33:09.080
Oscar, he trained me when I started here.

2099
01:33:09.560 --> 01:33:10.620
Kevin, really?

2100
01:33:10.620 --> 01:33:12.040
Kelly, Oscar nods.

2101
01:33:12.160 --> 01:33:12.980
And Kevin smiles.

2102
01:33:13.740 --> 01:33:16.160
What a sweet ending to that story.

2103
01:33:16.420 --> 01:33:16.800
I know.

2104
01:33:16.820 --> 01:33:19.100
And who is Todd at Vance Refrigeration?

2105
01:33:19.380 --> 01:33:23.980
I mean, it kind of sounded like maybe Todd used to work at Dunder Mifflin.

2106
01:33:24.160 --> 01:33:24.780
I don't know.

2107
01:33:24.780 --> 01:33:26.400
I feel like Todd is training people.

2108
01:33:27.300 --> 01:33:27.540
Yeah.

2109
01:33:28.180 --> 01:33:30.040
Anyway, I thought that was sort of sweet.

2110
01:33:30.340 --> 01:33:35.280
Well, speaking of sweet, something we haven't talked about is that cutie pie scene at reception

2111
01:33:35.280 --> 01:33:40.120
with Andy and Erin, where they're both not asking each other out on a date.

2112
01:33:40.660 --> 01:33:42.100
It's a totally adorable scene.

2113
01:33:42.420 --> 01:33:48.180
But if I'm being honest, I was very distracted by the fact that Pam's watercolor is back on the wall.

2114
01:33:48.280 --> 01:33:49.000
It's all I could see.

2115
01:33:49.800 --> 01:33:50.780
It's all I could see.

2116
01:33:50.860 --> 01:33:51.840
I was just so delighted.

2117
01:33:52.760 --> 01:33:55.180
Michael's going to return, and he's going to make a toaSt.

2118
01:33:55.400 --> 01:33:57.000
He's brought a lot of orange juice.

2119
01:33:57.040 --> 01:33:59.660
I guess in honor of Tallahassee.

2120
01:33:59.940 --> 01:34:00.380
Florida.

2121
01:34:00.760 --> 01:34:00.980
Yeah.

2122
01:34:01.280 --> 01:34:01.720
Oranges.

2123
01:34:02.220 --> 01:34:03.400
He makes his toaSt.

2124
01:34:03.400 --> 01:34:04.580
It's not really a toaSt.

2125
01:34:04.760 --> 01:34:08.000
But everyone takes a big swig, and they're like, ugh.

2126
01:34:08.600 --> 01:34:13.680
Yeah, I mean, have you ever had orange juice out of, like, an aluminum container?

2127
01:34:14.040 --> 01:34:14.820
A little tangy.

2128
01:34:15.360 --> 01:34:18.960
Yeah, I mean, it goes right into your teeth, just like they talk about.

2129
01:34:19.200 --> 01:34:20.160
But you know what?

2130
01:34:20.160 --> 01:34:24.540
I don't think that a lot of people are putting their orange juice into metal containers.

2131
01:34:24.540 --> 01:34:27.000
You know, it's really meant to be a water vessel.

2132
01:34:27.440 --> 01:34:28.900
I like that you said vessel.

2133
01:34:29.160 --> 01:34:29.680
Thank you.

2134
01:34:29.920 --> 01:34:31.180
It's a water vessel.

2135
01:34:31.340 --> 01:34:31.920
It is.

2136
01:34:32.160 --> 01:34:34.960
Well, I guess this toast means that Michael is going to stay.

2137
01:34:35.160 --> 01:34:36.720
He's going to stick it out at Saber.

2138
01:34:36.900 --> 01:34:37.380
He is.

2139
01:34:37.380 --> 01:34:41.580
He's going to, I guess, have to abide by all these new rules that he hates.

2140
01:34:41.760 --> 01:34:43.940
No YouTube for Michael at work?

2141
01:34:44.180 --> 01:34:45.580
I don't know how that's going to go.

2142
01:34:45.820 --> 01:34:50.360
The episode ends with David Wallace and his son jamming out to a song that he's written

2143
01:34:50.360 --> 01:34:51.200
called Suck It.

2144
01:34:51.360 --> 01:34:52.520
Let's listen to it.

2145
01:35:29.070 --> 01:35:30.910
Oh, and Daniel's on the druMs.

2146
01:35:30.950 --> 01:35:31.730
I know, man.

2147
01:35:32.270 --> 01:35:36.410
We got a fan question from Donna in Newport, New Virginia.

2148
01:35:36.830 --> 01:35:41.230
Please tell us everything about the Suck It song at the end of the episode,

2149
01:35:41.230 --> 01:35:43.110
How Much Was Improvised.

2150
01:35:43.490 --> 01:35:49.970
I will tell you, Donna, the lyrics to Suck It were written by Warren and Halstead.

2151
01:35:50.130 --> 01:35:53.370
These guys really were our go-to guys for song lyrics.

2152
01:35:54.030 --> 01:35:58.110
And then I reached out to Andy Buckley, who plays David Wallace.

2153
01:35:58.430 --> 01:36:03.450
He said that Warren and Halstead faxed him the lyrics the night before.

2154
01:36:04.050 --> 01:36:05.530
Old tech alert, Angela.

2155
01:36:05.810 --> 01:36:06.210
Fax.

2156
01:36:06.390 --> 01:36:06.690
Yeah.

2157
01:36:06.930 --> 01:36:07.930
They faxed them.

2158
01:36:08.330 --> 01:36:09.830
He went over them that night.

2159
01:36:09.970 --> 01:36:11.990
He went over them at lunch the next day.

2160
01:36:12.210 --> 01:36:15.330
That's when he was able to kind of fiddle around on a keyboard.

2161
01:36:15.730 --> 01:36:20.090
Andy said he only knew about three chords on a keyboard.

2162
01:36:20.430 --> 01:36:21.810
But he did his beSt.

2163
01:36:22.030 --> 01:36:24.730
They did not shoot this until the very end of the day.

2164
01:36:24.830 --> 01:36:27.870
They only had three minutes before they went into overtime.

2165
01:36:27.870 --> 01:36:29.370
Three minutes.

2166
01:36:29.650 --> 01:36:29.950
Yes.

2167
01:36:30.210 --> 01:36:31.350
No messing up.

2168
01:36:31.510 --> 01:36:31.990
Yes.

2169
01:36:32.210 --> 01:36:35.990
He said it all came together when Owen started playing the druMs.

2170
01:36:36.250 --> 01:36:37.730
It was kind of a magic moment.

2171
01:36:38.150 --> 01:36:39.870
They got it in three takes.

2172
01:36:40.270 --> 01:36:43.090
He said that the whole shoot day was a total blaSt.

2173
01:36:43.250 --> 01:36:44.850
He loved John as a director.

2174
01:36:45.170 --> 01:36:47.990
He said John just had tons of positive energy.

2175
01:36:48.590 --> 01:36:51.110
And that John and Jen Salata worked so well together.

2176
01:36:51.350 --> 01:36:54.590
He sort of remembered that someone would do a take,

2177
01:36:54.590 --> 01:36:56.770
and then you'd see them kind of huddle and giggle,

2178
01:36:56.950 --> 01:36:58.950
and then they would come over with some great idea.

2179
01:36:59.110 --> 01:37:00.190
That's my memory, too.

2180
01:37:00.390 --> 01:37:00.650
Same.

2181
01:37:01.070 --> 01:37:02.850
Andy said that inevitably,

2182
01:37:03.470 --> 01:37:07.850
if he is walking through an airport or some public place,

2183
01:37:08.070 --> 01:37:10.370
there is always someone who yells out,

2184
01:37:10.510 --> 01:37:11.150
suck it!

2185
01:37:13.150 --> 01:37:20.940
He said that he and Zach Woods are now on an HBO comedy called Avenue 5,

2186
01:37:20.980 --> 01:37:22.760
and it films in London.

2187
01:37:23.000 --> 01:37:23.600
Amazing.

2188
01:37:23.600 --> 01:37:26.980
He said they were in London for four months before the pandemic

2189
01:37:26.980 --> 01:37:28.940
in the summer of 2019,

2190
01:37:28.940 --> 01:37:32.620
and they just went back this past fall in 2021.

2191
01:37:33.500 --> 01:37:38.300
He said that the difference between people stopping the two of them

2192
01:37:38.300 --> 01:37:41.880
before the pandemic and after the pandemic is striking.

2193
01:37:42.380 --> 01:37:46.740
He said clearly a lot of people binged the office during the pandemic.

2194
01:37:47.000 --> 01:37:49.600
He's getting way more suckets lately.

2195
01:37:50.540 --> 01:37:52.300
Well, I hope that's a good thing.

2196
01:37:52.500 --> 01:37:53.180
I think so.

2197
01:37:53.800 --> 01:37:55.620
Well, that is the Saber episode.

2198
01:37:55.900 --> 01:37:57.160
Well, before we go,

2199
01:37:57.380 --> 01:38:00.900
I thought we could end this episode with a few final thoughts from John

2200
01:38:00.900 --> 01:38:04.100
about what directing this episode meant to him.

2201
01:38:04.940 --> 01:38:07.660
This show was my film school.

2202
01:38:07.920 --> 01:38:10.980
I mean, I was a waiter before I got the show.

2203
01:38:11.160 --> 01:38:13.560
I had zero experience in this business,

2204
01:38:13.920 --> 01:38:17.360
in anything, acting, writing, directing, producing, any of that stuff.

2205
01:38:17.360 --> 01:38:20.340
And there it was right in front of me.

2206
01:38:20.580 --> 01:38:29.360
I was somehow lucky enough to drop right into the most immense pool of talent that I could ever ask for.

2207
01:38:29.360 --> 01:38:35.260
From that insane group of people that we had as our writers,

2208
01:38:35.260 --> 01:38:39.680
a group of people that hadn't been assembled like that in a long, long time, I guess.

2209
01:38:39.860 --> 01:38:42.400
And, you know, in a lot of ways, probably hasn't been assembled since.

2210
01:38:42.400 --> 01:38:49.000
So, from writing, I got to understand what it meant to not only write well,

2211
01:38:49.240 --> 01:38:53.560
but to have fun with it and to have this drive to do something great.

2212
01:38:53.560 --> 01:38:59.000
I remember going up to the writers' rooms every now and again if I had a break between scenes or something

2213
01:38:59.000 --> 01:39:02.640
and, you know, watch them all come up with an idea.

2214
01:39:02.920 --> 01:39:06.620
And the idea was almost like you could visualize a balloon in the air.

2215
01:39:06.880 --> 01:39:10.080
And 12 to 14 people were making sure it didn't touch the ground before

2216
01:39:10.080 --> 01:39:14.200
they had investigated every single possibility of how to make it work

2217
01:39:14.200 --> 01:39:17.740
and how to make a joke work or an idea work.

2218
01:39:18.220 --> 01:39:20.640
And even if it didn't end up working,

2219
01:39:20.800 --> 01:39:22.740
they still put it on a different board that was like,

2220
01:39:22.740 --> 01:39:25.540
well, it's not working today, but maybe it'll work again on another day.

2221
01:39:25.740 --> 01:39:30.360
And if it did work, they all worked together to try to figure out

2222
01:39:30.360 --> 01:39:33.160
how it would best be used in this show.

2223
01:39:33.600 --> 01:39:38.660
So, writing-wise, there I was, front row seats in the greatest room I could be.

2224
01:39:39.160 --> 01:39:43.040
Visually, I had no idea what even a camera was.

2225
01:39:43.140 --> 01:39:45.540
I didn't know what the difference between lenses were.

2226
01:39:45.800 --> 01:39:48.740
I didn't know what type of cameras did what.

2227
01:39:49.000 --> 01:39:52.140
But I remember there again, the office was shot.

2228
01:39:52.740 --> 01:39:53.440
Single camera.

2229
01:39:53.660 --> 01:39:57.040
It was one of the first shows to be done like that in a long time.

2230
01:39:57.260 --> 01:40:00.060
And so, we were shooting the show like a movie.

2231
01:40:00.380 --> 01:40:06.120
So, I got to pick the brain of Randall and Matt and all these hugely talented people

2232
01:40:06.120 --> 01:40:11.820
who would explain to me everything from lenses to the aesthetic and lighting

2233
01:40:11.820 --> 01:40:14.880
and all these different things that I had absolutely no idea about.

2234
01:40:14.880 --> 01:40:20.380
After directing Saber, I used to hang out with Dave Rogers and watch him cut all these different episodes.

2235
01:40:20.560 --> 01:40:24.760
I'd pop in and see how he was making all these scenes work, how he was rearranging story,

2236
01:40:24.980 --> 01:40:29.960
how he was taking air out of a moment to make it work so much stronger.

2237
01:40:30.960 --> 01:40:37.800
So, really, at the end of the day, I certainly would not be writing and directing at all if it wasn't for this show.

2238
01:40:37.800 --> 01:40:40.280
I just, I wouldn't have had the confidence to do it.

2239
01:40:40.440 --> 01:40:46.100
So, I guess what I'm trying to say is I owe absolutely everything to this show.

2240
01:40:46.740 --> 01:40:50.540
Directing this episode was so very special.

2241
01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:53.420
And I will truly never forget it.

2242
01:40:53.920 --> 01:40:55.540
Thank you for having me on the show.

2243
01:40:55.920 --> 01:40:59.660
I was so excited to come back and have me back again.

2244
01:41:00.240 --> 01:41:01.940
But again, like I said, slow down.

2245
01:41:02.140 --> 01:41:03.260
We need more of this podcaSt.

2246
01:41:03.500 --> 01:41:03.660
Please.

2247
01:41:04.400 --> 01:41:06.260
John Krasinski, we love you.

2248
01:41:06.500 --> 01:41:07.900
Love you so much, John.

2249
01:41:08.080 --> 01:41:09.920
That was so generous of you.

2250
01:41:10.120 --> 01:41:11.500
You shared so much with us.

2251
01:41:11.660 --> 01:41:13.880
And I just loved hearing your voice.

2252
01:41:14.180 --> 01:41:14.700
Same.

2253
01:41:15.160 --> 01:41:21.160
Also want to give a big thank you to Yaki Margulies for that amazing Beach Boys parody.

2254
01:41:21.560 --> 01:41:26.980
You can find him on Twitter and Instagram at Yaki Margulies.

2255
01:41:27.200 --> 01:41:30.740
And you can listen to his music online anywhere that you get your music,

2256
01:41:30.740 --> 01:41:33.420
like Spotify, Apple Music, iTunes, etc.

2257
01:41:34.060 --> 01:41:39.580
I also want to say thank you to Joya Balfour for sharing with us what NBC Digital was up to.

2258
01:41:39.900 --> 01:41:43.460
And to Randy Cordray and Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan.

2259
01:41:43.780 --> 01:41:45.160
Thank you, everyone.

2260
01:41:45.520 --> 01:41:47.740
We will see you next week.

2261
01:41:48.080 --> 01:41:50.440
I hope you enjoy a fluffernutter this week.

2262
01:41:50.720 --> 01:41:51.900
I'm going to have one.

2263
01:41:52.300 --> 01:41:53.120
Please do.

2264
01:41:53.480 --> 01:41:54.160
I will.

2265
01:41:54.420 --> 01:41:54.660
Okay.

2266
01:41:54.880 --> 01:41:55.900
Oh, on gluten-free bread.

2267
01:41:56.380 --> 01:41:56.740
Yay.

2268
01:42:02.500 --> 01:42:04.460
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

2269
01:42:04.740 --> 01:42:08.660
Office Ladies is produced by Earwolf, Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.

2270
01:42:08.660 --> 01:42:11.600
